{{smaller block|It was surely impossible any man's reason should tell him the particular circumstances of the world's creation, as that its material principal was a ''tohu'' and ''bohu'', that it was agitated by the divine spirit, that severall portions were form'd at severall times, that all was finished in six dayes space, etc. {{right|''Bp. Parker'', Platonick Phil., p. 85.}}}}

'''toil'''<sup>1</sup> (toil), ''v.'' [Early mod. E. also ''toyle''; < ME. ''toilen'', ''toylen'' (Sc. ''toilze'', ''tulze''), appar. < OF. ''toiller'', ''touiller'', ''toouiller'', ''teouiller'', F. ''touiller'', mix, entangle, trouble, besmear; origin unknown. Cf. ''toil''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'' The sense 'labor, till' appears to be due in part to association with ''till''<sup>1</sup> (ME. ''tillen'', ''tilen'', ''tolen'', ''tulien'', etc.), and the form is near to that of MD. ''tuylen'', ''teulen'', till, labor (see ''till''<sup>1</sup>); but the AS. verb could not produce an E. form ''toil'', and a ME. verb of such general import could hardly be derived from MD. The sense 'pull' may be due in part to association with ''till''<sup>3</sup>, ''toll''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.'''† ''trans.'' '''1.''' To pull about; tug; drag.

{{smaller block|The dispitous Iewes nolde not spare
Til trie [choice] fruit weore tore and ''toyled''.
{{right|''Holy Rood'' (ed. Morris), p. 143.}}}}

{{smaller block|His syre a soutere, . . .
His teeth with ''toyling'' of lether tatered [jagged] as a sawe!
{{right|''Piers Plowman's Crede'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 753.}}}}

'''2.''' To harass; weary or exhaust by toil: often used reflexively (whence later, by omission of the reflexive pronoun, the intransitive use): sometimes with ''out''.

{{smaller block|For some paltry gaine,
He digs, & delves, & ''toiels'' himselfe with paine.
{{right|''Times' Whistle'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 118.}}}}

{{smaller block|I am weary and ''toiled'' with rowing up and down in the seas of questions. ''Jer. Taylor'', Great Exemplar, Ded., p. 4.}}

'''3.''' To labor; work; till.

{{smaller block|Places well ''toiled'' and husbanded. ''Holland.'' (''Imp. Dict.'')}}

readings.c2s1:
All things have rest; why should we ''toil'' alone,<br>We only ''toil'', who are the first of things?
{{right|''Tennyson'', Lotos Eaters, Choric Song.}}

2. To move or travel with difficulty, weariness, or pain.

{{smaller block|The king of men, by Juno's self inspir'd,<br>''Toil'd'' through the tents, and all his army fir'd.
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, viii. 267.}}}}

{{smaller block|Slow ''toiling'' upward from the misty vale,<br>I leave the bright enamelled zones below.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Nearing the Snow-Line.}}}}

='''Syn.''' 1. To drudge, moil, strive. See the noun.

'''toil'''<sup>1</sup> (toil), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''toyle''; < ME. ''toil, toile, toyle'' (Sc. ''tuilye, tuilue, toolye'', etc.); from the verb.] 1†. Confusion; turmoil; uproar; struggle; tussle.

{{smaller block|Troilus, in the ''toile'', turnyt was of hors,<br>Ffaght vppon fote felly agayne.
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 6550.}}}}

{{smaller block|And whan these com on ther was so grete ''toile'' and romour of noyse that wonder it was to heere, and therwith a-roos so grete a duste that the cleir sky wax all derk.
{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 393.}}}}

2. Harassing labor; labor accompanied with fatigue and pain; exhausting effort.

{{smaller block|Pleasure 's a ''Toil'' when constantly pursu'd.
{{right|''Congreve'', tr. of Eleventh Satire of Juvenal.}}}}

{{smaller block|Sic as you and I,<br>Wha drudge and drive through wet and dry,<br>Wi' never ceasing ''toil''.
{{right|''Burns'', First Epistle to Davie.}}}}

{{smaller block|It 's been a long ''toil'' for thee all this way in the heat, with thy child.
{{right|''Mrs. Gaskell'', Sylvia's Lovers, xxxvi.}}}}

3. A work accomplished; an achievement.

readings.c2s3:
¶
'''toile''' (twol), ''n.'' [F.: see ''toil''<sup>2</sup>.] Cloth: used in some technical names.—{{smaller|'''Toile cirée,''' oil-cloth, especially that which is of very fine or rare quality: the French term, often used in English.—'''Toile colbert,''' a kind of canvas used for embroidery: same as ''connaught.'' ''Dict. of Needlework.''—'''Toile d'Alsace,''' a thin linen cloth used for women's summer dresses. Compare ''toile de Vichy.''—'''Toile de religieuse.''' Same as ''nun's-cloth'' or ''nun's-veiling.''—'''Toile de Vichy,''' a linen material used for summer dresses for women, generally having a simple striped pattern. ''Dict. of Needlework.''}}

'''toilé''' (F. pron. two-lā′), ''n.'' [F., < ''toile,'' cloth: see ''toil''<sup>2</sup>.] In ''lace-making,'' the closely worked or mat part of the pattern; hence, the pattern in general, as distinguished from the ground.

'''toiler''' (toi′lėr), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''toyler;'' < ''toil''<sup>1</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who toils; one who labors in a wearying or unremitting manner.

{{smaller block|I will not pray for those goodes in getting and heaping together whereof the ''toylers'' of the worlde thinke themselfes fortunate.
{{right|''Udall,'' On Pet. i.}}}}

'''toilet, toilette''' (toi′let, toi-let′), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. also ''toylet;'' < OF. ''toilette,'' a cloth, a bag to put clothes in, F. ''toilette,'' a toilet, dressing-table, dressing-apparatus, dressing-gown, wrapper, dress, dim. of ''toile,'' cloth: see ''toil''<sup>2</sup>.] 1†. A cloth, generally of linen.

{{smaller block|Toilette. . . . A ''Toylet,'' the stuffe which Drapers lap about their clothes.
{{right|''Cotgrave.''}}}}

readings.c2s4:
Hence—2†. An article made of linen or other cloth. (''a'') A cloth to be thrown over the shoulders during shaving or hair-dressing.

{{smaller block|Pleasant was the answer of Archelaus to the barber, who, after he had cast the linnen ''toylet'' about his shoulders, put this question to him: How shall I trim your Majesty? Without any more prating, quoth the king.
{{right|''Plutarch'', Morals (trans.), iv. 232. (''Latham.'')}}}}

(''b'') A cover for a dressing-table, or for the articles set upon it. Now called ''toilet-cover''.

{{smaller block|''Toilet'', a kind of Table-cloth, or Carpet, made of fine Linnen, Sattin, Velvet, or Tissue, spread upon a Table in a Bed-Chamber, where Persons of Quality dress themselves; a Dressing-cloth.
{{right|''E. Phillips'', World of Words, 1706.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller|(c)}} A bag or cloth case for holding clothing, etc.

{{smaller block|Toilette. . . . A ''Toylet'', . . . a bag to put night-clothes and buckeram, or other stuffe to wrap any other clothes in.
{{right|''Cotgrave'', 1611.}}}}

Hence—3†. The articles, collectively, used in dressing, as a mirror, bottles, boxes, brushes, and combs, set upon the dressing-table; a toilet-service.

{{smaller block|The greate looking-glasse and ''toilet'' of beaten and massive gold was given by the Queene Mother.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, June 9, 1662.}}

And now, unveil'd, the ''toilet'' stands display'd,<br>Each silver vase in mystic order laid.
{{right|''Pope'', R. of the L., i. 121.}}}}

4. A dressing-table furnished with a mirror: more commonly called ''toilet-table.''

{{smaller block|Plays, operas, circles, I no more must view!<br>My ''toilette'', patches, all the world, adieu!
{{right|''Lady M. W. Montagu'', Town Eclogues, vi.}}

The lieutenant folded his arms, and, leaning against the ''toilet'', sunk into a reverie.
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, I. 15.}}}}

5. The process of dressing; formerly, specifically, the dressing and powdering of the hair, during which women of fashion received callers.

{{smaller block|I'll carry you into Company; Mr. Fainlove, you shall introduce him to Mrs. Clerimont's ''Toilet.''
{{right|''Steele'', Tender Husband, i. 1.}}

The merchant from th' Exchange returns in peace,<br>And the long labours of the ''toilet'' cease.
{{right|''Pope'', R. of the L., iii. 24.}}

His best blue suit . . . he wore with becoming calmness; having, after a little wrangling, effected what was always the one point of interest to him in his ''toilette''—he had transferred all the contents of his every-day pockets to those actually in wear.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Mill on the Floss, i. 9.}}}}

